HST PDF: say why a signature is invalid, and show its claimed signer and time as not verified.

The verdict does not change; only the explanation is new.

The signer was missing for most invalid signatures. ValidateSignature returned before reading
the CMS when an earlier check failed, for example the check of updates after signing, so the
certificate and the signing time were never read (cryptosign records the certificate before the
digest check, so the CMS is not the problem). Those paths now read the signer, the signing time
and the digest algorithm from the CMS into a separate SignatureInformation and copy only those
fields, never the status. For an invalid signature these values are what the CMS claims, not
verified: HST PDF labels them "Ký bởi (chưa xác minh)" and "Thời điểm ký (chưa xác minh)".

Byte ranges are bounded on every path: more than 16 ranges, or ranges whose lengths add up to
more than the file, are never hashed (Signing::Verify allocates their sum), and the signature
stays invalid. When the signature itself (the gap between the ranges) still lies inside the file,
its CMS is read against no content, which gives the claimed signer and time but can never match;
when it lies outside the file, nothing is read.

Certificates of invalid signatures are not checked: GetDocumentSignatureInformations and the
signatures dialog (for PDF) call verifyCertificate only for a signature that verifies, so a file
cannot make HST fetch issuer or revocation data for a certificate it merely claims. The
certificate status of an invalid signature stays INVALID, as before.

PDFSignatureHelper::GetSignatureProblems() gives the reason for each signature:
- Broken: malformed or too many byte ranges, an unsupported sub-filter, or an unreadable CMS.
- Rewritten: the bytes between the signed ranges are no longer the hex /Contents string, or the
  file ends before them (the file was written again after signing).
- Incomplete: the signature is in place but the file ends before the signed data does, e.g. a
  download that did not finish.
- ModifiedAfterSigning: the signed bytes are intact, but a later update changes the pages in a way
  the signature does not allow.
- ContentChanged: the structure is intact, but the bytes no longer match the signature.

Signing a file whose page tree HST cannot update incrementally (objects in AES-encrypted object
streams, as iText writes them) used to fail with "check the USB token and the PIN". signPdf now
returns SignResult::Unsupported for it, and HST PDF says that the file's structure (usually its
encryption) cannot be updated yet and that the file is unchanged.

HST PDF shows the reason under each signature in the signature panel. The status bar shows
"Chữ ký số không hợp lệ: <reason>" when every invalid signature fails for the same reason, and its
tooltip lists the invalid signatures. HSTPdfInfo also reports SignatureLines, SignatureProblems
and SignatureDetails. An untrusted certificate keeps today's wording.

